  1317		Let $\mu$ be a finitely supported probability distribution on a sample
  1318		type, let $G$ be a decidable event of positive mass $p = \mu(G) > 0$, and
  1319		write $\mu \mid G$ for the distribution $\mu$ conditioned on $G$. Then,
  1320		for every real-valued $f$,
  1321		\begin{equation}
  1322			\label{eq:restricted-average-normalization}
  1323			p \cdot \E_{\mu \mid G} f = \sum_{s \in G} \mu(s) f(s)\,,
  1324		\end{equation}
  1325		and if moreover $f(s) \le 1$ for every sample $s$,
  1326		\begin{equation}
  1327			\label{eq:restricted-average-restoration}
  1328			\E_{\mu} f \le p \cdot \E_{\mu \mid G} f + \mu(G^{c})\,.
  1329		\end{equation}
  1330		The mass $\mu(G^{c})$ discarded by the conditioning is retained in full in
  1331		\eqref{eq:restricted-average-restoration}; no term is dropped.
  1332	\end{lemma}
  1333	\begin{proof}
  1334		\leanok
  1335		The conditioned weights are $\mu(s)/p$ on $G$ and $0$ off $G$, which gives
  1336		\eqref{eq:restricted-average-normalization} termwise. For
  1337		\eqref{eq:restricted-average-restoration}, split the sum defining
  1338		$\E_\mu f$ along $G$ and its complement: the first part is the right-hand
  1339		side of \eqref{eq:restricted-average-normalization}, and the second is at
  1340		most $\mu(G^{c})$ because the weights are nonnegative and $f \le 1$.
  1341	\end{proof}
